{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When a man shall have in the skin of his flesh, etc.</b> The parashah of nega'im is placed adjacent to the preceding parashah, which speaks of circumcision, to tell you that circumcision pushes aside nega'im, for in every case the commandment that comes first pushes aside the one that comes later, as explained above in parashat Vayakhel on the words of our Sages, of blessed memory: He placed Shabbat before the construction of the Mishkan, to tell you that Shabbat pushes aside the work of the Mishkan. And by way of allusion we may explain: our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Nedarim 31b) — great is circumcision, for it pushes aside nega'im — because circumcision in general is the cause for the circumcision of all four foreskins, and included among them is ערלת הפה, the foreskin of the lips, and when a man is circumcised in the foreskin of his lips he will not speak lashon ha-ra, and then he will not come to nega'im, which come upon lashon ha-ra (Arakhin 15b). And on this Moses quarreled, as it were, in saying (Exodus 5:23): ״וּמֵאָז בָּאתִי לְדַבֵּר בִּשְׁמֶךָ״ and so on — according to the view of the midrash (Yalkut Shimoni Exodus 174), which says: all the dealings of Moses were with 'אָז' and so on — an allusion to circumcision. And likewise it concludes explicitly in the Yalkut, parashat Beshalach (remez 284, section 15) on the verse ״אָז יָשִׁיר מֹשֶׁה״, that the sea was split in the merit of circumcision, which is given on the eighth day, corresponding to the numerical value of 'אָז'. And likewise all those instances of 'אָז' that the midrash Devarim Rabbah mentions on the verse ״אָז יַבְדִּיל מֹשֶׁה״ (Deuteronomy 4:41), for in that too there is an allusion to circumcision, which shields from the shedding of blood, as explained above in parashat Bo on the verse ״רְאוּ כִּי רָעָה נֶגֶד פְּנֵיכֶם״ (Exodus 10:10). And likewise the saving of his life was only through 'אָז', as it says (Exodus 4:26): ״אָז אָמְרָה חֲתַן דָּמִים לַמּוּלֹת״. And likewise his estrangement [from his wife] was only through 'אָז', as it says (Exodus 6:23): ״וּמֵאָז בָּאתִי אֶל פַּרְעֹה לְדַבֵּר בִּשְׁמֶךָ״ — Moses said: I know that I am עַרַל שְׂפָתַיִם, and I did not come to speak in Your name except from the time I relied upon the circumcision alluded to in 'אָז', which would be a cause also for the repair of ערלת הפה, and I see that it availed nothing." "<b>A swelling, or a scab, or a bright spot, etc.</b> Regarding the nature of tzara'at there are differing opinions: some say it is a natural disease hidden within the body that breaks out to the surface, and some say that it all comes by way of punishment to reveal the evil hidden within him. In either case, it seems to me [fitting] to explain the word מְצֹרָע as a compound word: מוֹצִיא רַע — one who brings out evil — for it reveals and brings out to the outside all the evil that is within him, whether the inner corruption, or whether he strikes his fellow secretly with the scourge of his tongue and people do not know to beware of him; therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, publicly exposes the flatterers and brings out all their evil to the outside, so that their wickedness may be revealed in the congregation. And with this the redundancy in the verse is resolved, where it says: ״אָדָם כִּי יִהְיֶה בְעוֹר בְּשָׂרוֹ שְׂאֵת וְגוֹ׳, וְהָיָה בְעוֹר בְּשָׂרוֹ לְנֶגַע צָרָעַת״ — why do I need בְעוֹר בְּשָׂרוֹ twice? Rather, to tell you that this swelling and this bright spot, had they been in a man of pure thoughts, would not have been harmful, for it would be easy to heal them; but because they are בְעוֹר בְּשָׂרוֹ — in the skin of the flesh — of this sinner, therefore they became a נֶגַע צָרָעַת. And from this the sages of our Torah found support, and most of them went out to gather from the Torah and from the Prophets and from the Writings for which particular sin nega'im come. For in tractate Arakhin (16a) our Sages, of blessed memory, said: for seven things nega'im come, and so on — and all of them have support from the verses. And in the midrash (Vayikra Rabbah 17:3) it brings for ten things, and so on. But we have no concern with the hidden matters from this parashah but only with what is revealed in it, and we find from all of them an allusion from these explicit verses to three things. The first is for lashon ha-ra, as Rashi explained: why is his offering birds? Because they engage in chirping and so on. The second is for arrogance, as it says: ״וְלָקַח לַמִּטַּהֵר עֵץ אֶרֶז וְאֵזוֹב״ — Rashi explained: if he exalted himself like a cedar, he should humble himself like hyssop. The third is for narrowness of eye [stinginess], as it says (Leviticus 14:35): ״וּבָא אֲשֶׁר לוֹ הַבַּיִת״, and the Tanna of the school of Rabbi Yishmael [said]: this is one who singled out his house for himself and so on (Arakhin 16a). And this matter encompasses several things: both the desire for money, which is learned from Gehazi, and robbery, which our Sages, of blessed memory, enumerated there — and their proof is from the verse: ״וְצִוָּה הַכֹּהֵן וּפִנּוּ אֶת הַבַּיִת״, and the Tanna [said]: he brought in money that was not his; the priest will come and scatter his money. And these three have a single source, for the desire for money causes both robbery and narrowness of eye." "And all the other things enumerated in the Gemara and in the midrashim have no support from this parashah; therefore it appeared to my eyes to establish all the details of the practices performed for the metzora on the foundation of these three, and they are: lashon ha-ra, and arrogance, and the desire for money — and their general principle is the verse that says: ״מְלָשְׁנִי בַסֵּתֶר רֵעֵהוּ אוֹתוֹ אַצְמִית גְּבַהּ עֵינַיִם וּרְחַב לֵבָב אוֹתוֹ לֹא אוּכָל״ (Psalms 101:5). ״וּרְחַב לֵבָב״ — that is the one who desires money, as they said (Avot 5:23) regarding Balaam, that he had a wide soul, and ״אוֹתוֹ אַצְמִית״ — our Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted (Arakhin 15b) regarding a confirmed metzora. And the verse makes two divisions to join גְּבַהּ עֵינַיִם with וּרְחַב לֵבָב, for both are from a single source — since 'the sight of the eye depends on the lens of the heart' (Avodah Zarah 28b), and every haughtiness of eyes is וּרְחַב לֵבָב, for all arrogance of spirit longs for abundance of wealth, for through it one can show the precious splendor of one's greatness; and on the other side, all wealth causes elevation of heart, as it is written (Deuteronomy 8:13–14): ״וְכֶסֶף וְזָהָב יִרְבֶּה לָּךְ וְגוֹ׳ וְרָם לְבָבֶךָ״ and so on. And a proof [comes] from what Elisha said to Gehazi: ״וְצָרַעַת נַעֲמָן תִּדְבַּק בְּךָ״ (II Kings 5:27) — why did he need to mention 'the tzara'at of Na'aman'? Rather, because Gehazi desired money (ibid. 5:20–24) and Na'aman was גְּבַהּ עֵינַיִם, and the commentators learned this from what is written (ibid. 5:1): ״וְנַעֲמָן שַׂר צְבָא מֶלֶךְ אֲרָם הָיָה אִישׁ גָּדוֹל לִפְנֵי אֲדֹנָיו״ — meaning that even before his master he conducted himself with greatness and pride. And we have already said that arrogance and desire stem from one place; therefore he said: ״וְצָרַעַת נַעֲמָן תִּדְבַּק בְּךָ״ — and so too David joined them in saying: ״גְּבַהּ עֵינַיִם וּרְחַב לֵבָב״ and so on." "Therefore it says: ״אָדָם כִּי יִהְיֶה בְעוֹר בְּשָׂרוֹ שְׂאֵת״ — שְׂאֵת means nothing but exaltedness, and this type of tzara'at comes upon arrogance of spirit that lifts itself above all to the head. ״אוֹ סַפַּחַת״ — סַפַּחַת means nothing but something attached, and so on — and it comes upon one who desires the acquisition of money, which is attached to a person from the outside and does not become intrinsic to the person at all, unlike other qualities. And so the Rambam wrote in his Eight Chapters: all acquisitions, such as wisdom and might and excellence of character traits, all become intrinsic to a person and are bound to him, except for wealth, for it is attached to a person from the outside and is not bound to him at all. ״אוֹ בַהֶרֶת״ — that is whiteness; this type comes upon one who speaks lashon ha-ra and whitens the face of his fellow and produces white patches on him, for 'redness departs and paleness comes' (Bava Metzi'a 58b), as it is written (Isaiah 29:22): ״לֹא עַתָּה יֵבוֹשׁ יַעֲקֹב וְלֹא עַתָּה פָּנָיו יֶחֱוָרוּ״. And in this way his facial appearance testifies against him — whether his inner being is packed with love of money, or arrogance, or speaking ill of his fellow — and this nega brings out his evil to the outside to reveal his wickedness in the congregation. And because of this he is called מְצֹרָע — מוֹצִיא רַע — in the manner that it says (Exodus 32:25): ״וַיַּרְא מֹשֶׁה אֶת הָעָם כִּי פָרֻעַ הוּא״, and our Sages, of blessed memory, said that they were stricken with tzara'at (Yalkut Shimoni, Tazria 14, remez 596) — as is found: ״וְרֹאשׁוֹ יִהְיֶה פָרוּעַ״ (Leviticus 13:45) — and that is an expression of disclosure, that his wickedness shall be disclosed and seen before the eyes of all. And that is the essence of tzara'at." "<b>And he shall be brought to the priest.</b> His purification is only through a priest, because everyone of the seed of Aaron possesses three good traits that are the direct opposites of these. For the sin of the tongue causes every quarrel and every nega and divides even between brothers; therefore Aaron comes, who held fast to the trait of peace, and he heals this one — for he was one who loved peace and pursued peace. And likewise the sin of arrogance is fitting to be rectified through him, for he was exceedingly humble, as our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Ḥullin 89a): greater is what is written of Moses and Aaron than what is written of Abraham, for regarding Abraham it is written (Genesis 18:27): ״וְאָנֹכִי עָפָר וָאֵפֶר״, but regarding Moses and Aaron it is written (Exodus 16:8): ״וְנַחְנוּ מָה״. And likewise the sin of the desire for money was also not in Aaron, for the priests had no portion or inheritance in the land, and they had nothing more than what they received from the table of the Most High; therefore they were complete in the trait of contentment and were not included among the narrow-eyed, whose entire aim is to gather and amass. Therefore it is fitting that all three types of tzara'at should not be purified except through a priest."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {4 "The phrase ״שׁוּרְיָינָא דְּעֵינָא בְּאוֹבְנָתָא דְּלִבָּא תָּלֵי״ is an Aramaic dictum cited from Avodah Zarah 28b; the precise anatomical referents are uncertain, rendered here hyper-literally as 'the sight of the eye depends on the lens of the heart.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:51.359802037Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}